<callAction triggerWith="$v1" target="$v1Credit" actionName="submitAnswer" />

<problem><title>Video 1 review question</title>
<p>Which of the following are valid descriptions of <m>m_{t+1}−m_t</m>? Hint: a lot of them are valid descriptions. You need to select all of them.</p>

  
  <answer matchPartial="true">
    <choiceInput selectMultiple="true">
 <choice name="c1" credit="1">(A) An expression for change in the moose population</choice>
 <choice name="c2" credit="1">(B) The quantity we need to compute to determine future moose population sizes from the initial condition </choice>
 <choice name="c3">(C) The initial condition for moose population size</choice>
 <choice name="c4" credit="1">(D) A quantity whose formula will give the rule for how the moose dynamical system changes with time</choice>
 <choice name="c5" credit="1">(E) An expression involving the state variables of the moose dynamical system</choice>
 <choice name="c6" credit="1">(F) The difference between the moose population sizes at year <m>t+1</m> and year <m>t</m></choice>
 <choice name="c7" credit="1">(G) A quantity that is positive if the moose population is growing</choice>
 <choice name="c8" credit="1">(H) A quantity that is negative if the moose population is shrinking</choice>
</choiceInput>
  </answer>


  
<feedback condition="$c1">(A) Yes, <m>m_{t+1}-m_t</m> is indeed an expression for change in the moose population, as it is the change from year <m>t</m> to year <m>t+1</m>.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$c2">(B) Yes, the quantity <m>m_{t+1}-m_t</m> (for each year <m>t</m>) is exactly what we need to compute <m>m_1</m> from <m>m_0</m> (when <m>t=0</m>), to compute <m>m_2</m> from <m>m_1</m> (when <m>t=1</m>).  In general, it allows us to compute <m>m_{t+1}</m> in year <m>t+1</m> from the previous year's population size of <m>m_t</m>.</feedback>
  <feedback condition="$c3">(C) No, the expression <m>m_{t+1}-m_{t}</m> is not an initial condition, but a change in population size.  An initial condition would be something like <m>m_0</m>, the population size in year 0.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$c4">(D) Yes, <m>m_{t+1} - m_t</m> does give the rule for how the moose dynamical system evolves, as it allows one to calculation <m>m_{t+1}</m>,  the population at year <m>t+1</m>, from <m>m_{t}</m>, the population in the previous year <m>t</m>.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$c5">(E) Yes, the state variable of the moose dynamical system is <m>m</m>.  The expression <m>m_{t+1}</m> is the value of this state variable in year <m>t+1</m>, and the expression <m>m_t</m> is the value of this state variable in year <m>t</m>.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$c6">(F) Yes, the expression <m>m_{t+1}-m_{t}</m> is exactly the difference between the moose population sizes in year <m>t+1</m> and year <m>t</m>.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$c7">(G) Indeed. If <m>m_{t+1}-m_{t} \gt 0</m>, then <m>m_{t+1} \gt m_t</m>.  In this case, the population size in year <m>t+1</m> is greater than the population size in the previous year <m>t</m>, and the population is growing.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$c8">(H) That is correct. If <m>m_{t+1}-m_{t} \lt 0</m>, then <m>m_{t+1} \lt m_t</m>.  In this case, the population size in year <m>t+1</m> is smaller than the population size in the previous year <m>t</m>, and the population is shrinking.</feedback>

  
</problem>
</section>

